## Session 80 — D4 closed. Grade: B (ambitious failure with structural reason).

**What I produced**
- New experiment: `experiments/quantum/szegedy_walk_prime_graphs/` — 4 scripts implementing Szegedy walks (Szegedy 2004, arxiv quant-ph/0401053) on three number-theoretic graph families (Cayley `(Z/NZ)*`, coprime, divisor).
- New EDGES.md entry **E7.13**: "Szegedy walks on arithmetic graphs do not yield polylog π(x)" — packages the joint-incompatibility (fast mixing AND primality eigenvector) into a negative-shape edge.
- E7.12 quantum-extension annotation: classical Cayley ω(n)-probing barrier carries to Szegedy quantisation.
- New CLOSED_PATHS row + ATTACK_VECTORS §D.D4 closure entry + session synthesis.

**Key empirical findings**
- **Cayley sweep** (14 primes N up to 1009): `t_classical ~ N^{0.789}`, `t_szegedy ~ N^{0.414}` — quadratic Szegedy speedup empirically realised but neither is polylog.
- **Coprime graph C_x**: spectral gap asymptotically constant `δ → 0.4166`; stationary prime mass / uniform → **ζ(2) = π²/6 ≈ 1.6449** (Mertens; deviation -0.022 at x=1000) — clean closed form.
- **Divisor graph D_x**: gap Ω(1); high-prime-mass eigenvectors localise on prime-centered divisor clusters (one specific prime + its multiples per eigenvector), not on global primality.

**Why structural**: Polylog primality extraction needs (i) `δ = Ω(1/polylog)` AND (ii) primality-localised eigenvector. The three families exhibit exhaustive incompatibility: Cayley fails (i), coprime/divisor fail (ii) beyond the trivial Perron mode.

**Cross-domain ingredient did real work**: discriminant matrix theorem applied; quadratic-speedup formula `t_Q ≈ sqrt(t_C)` verified empirically (slope ratio 0.524 ≈ 0.5).

