    752	| §D.D4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Szegedy quantum walks on number-theoretic graphs (cross-domain: Szegedy 2004 discriminant matrix theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether Szegedy walks on a number-theoretic graph yield polylog primality extraction. Tested **three families** with the discriminant matrix `D(x,y) = sqrt(P(x,y)·P(y,x))` and the eigenvalue-lifting theorem `cos(θ_k) → e^{±2iθ_k}` (mixing time `O(1/sqrt(δ))`). **(A) Cayley `Cay((Z/NZ)*, {2,3,5,inv})`** for primes N∈{31,…,1009} (14 sizes): empirical `t_classical(N) ~ N^{0.789}`, `t_szegedy(N) ~ N^{0.414}` — quadratic Szegedy speedup empirically realised but neither is polylog. Direct **quantum extension of E7.12 (S79)**: classical Cayley spectrum probes ω(n) not primality, AND Szegedy quantisation inherits the same poly-mixing barrier. **(B) Coprime `C_x = ([1..x], gcd=1)`** for x∈{30,…,1000}: spectral gap is `Ω(1)` (asymptotically `δ → 0.4166...`); stationary prime mass / uniform prime mass `→ ζ(2) = π²/6 ≈ 1.6449` (Mertens; verified at x=1000 with deviation -0.022). Both classical and Szegedy mix in `O(1)`, but bias is constant — `O(log x)` samples to hit a prime + AKS verification = no polylog extractor of π(x). **(C) Divisor `D_x = ([1..x], m|n)`**: gap `~Ω(1)` (slowly decaying 0.21 → 0.166 across x=30..1000). High-prime-mass eigenvectors localise on **prime-centered divisor clusters** `{p, 2p, 3p, ..., 1}` for *individual* primes near x/3-x/2 (e.g., k=14 eigenvector at x=250 is concentrated 100% on {43, 47} ∪ multiples ∪ {1}). Each eigenvector identifies 1-2 specific primes — same structural mechanism as E7.12 (degree class probing), not global primality. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: empirical confirmation `t_Q ≈ sqrt(t_C)` for the Szegedy-Cayley case validates the discriminant theorem at the project's scale; the π²/6 stationary identity is a clean Mertens-law derivation specific to the coprime-graph chain. **Falsification check**: no graph yields mixing time `O(polylog(x))` AND a primality-localised eigenvector with poly-log gap; both Cayley (poly mixing) and coprime/divisor (Ω(1) mixing but no global primality eigenvector) miss the joint condition. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §D.D4. **Suggested EDGE** (E7.13 candidate): "Szegedy-walk quantisation of any reversible chain on `[1..x]` with adjacency defined by simple arithmetic relation does not yield polylog primality extraction — either spectral gap shrinks polynomially (Cayley class) or eigenvectors capture primality only via degree-class clustering (coprime / divisor class). The two conditions are exhaustive on the families tested." Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10, E7.12. Cross-domain reference: Szegedy 2004 (arxiv quant-ph/0401053). See experiments/quantum/szegedy_walk_prime_graphs/. | 80 |
    753	| C6 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES) — three-pillars × HKM time-space tradeoff classification | REFINEMENT | I | Composition of E7.7 (three-pillars informational closure: prime positions / zeta zeros / floor values) and E6.7 (HKM at (T = N^{8/15}, S = N^{1/3})). Built (alpha, beta) catalog of 14 algorithms (4 prime / 4 zeta / 6 floor) and computed per-pillar Pareto frontiers, cross-pillar dominance, and saturation against E7.6 lower bound (T*S ≥ Ω(N^{5/6}/log N)). **Findings**: (i) HKM is on the floor-pillar Pareto frontier and DOMINATES every other floor-pillar entry elementwise (Lucy DP, Lucy+Fenwick, Meissel-Lehmer classical, Gourdon, primecount); (ii) HKM has NO cross-pillar dominators — its (8/15, 1/3) point is uniquely accessible on the floor pillar (no zero-pillar or prime-pillar entry achieves both T ≤ N^{8/15} AND S ≤ N^{1/3}); (iii) **pillar dominance regions are non-overlapping**: time-only minimum is shared by prime/zeta pillars at α=1/2, space-only minimum is unique to floor at β=1/3, T*S minimum is unique to floor at 13/15 ≈ 0.867; (iv) HKM saturates E7.6 to within N^{0.034} (gap 13/15 - 5/6 = 1/30); (v) Aggarwal binary search (E6.6) is a meta-algorithm whose effective placement migrates with the chosen pi(x) sub-routine. All four pre-stated falsification criteria (F1: HKM dominated; F2: HKM cross-pillar dominated; F3: identical pillar frontiers; F4: floor-pillar T*S < 5/6) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) explicit pillar-by-pillar Pareto map of the project's catalog; (b) HKM uniqueness lemma (HKM's (T, S) point is achievable on the floor pillar ONLY); (c) saturation verdict (floor pillar asymptotically tight). No polylog opening — refinement of E7.7 and E6.7, not a new edge. Cites E5.1, E6.5, E6.6, E6.7, E6.8, E6.9, E7.6, E7.7, E7.9. See experiments/constructions/pillar_tradeoff_diagram/. | 81 |
